<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patanjali</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Patañjali, Gonardiya, Gonikaputra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patanjali is the name of one or more authors, mystics and philosophers of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to whom several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works are attributed. The best known of them is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a> Sutras, a classical yoga text whose author may have lived between the 2nd century BCE and the 5th century CE. A Patanjali also wrote the Mahābhāṣya, a treatise on Sanskrit grammar firmly datable to the 2nd century BCE, and medical texts are credited to the name. No known text before the time of Bhoja in the 11th century conflates the two authors, and Western scholars now generally accept that they were different people; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition Patanjali is regarded as an avatar of Adi Sesha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patanjali.jpg" alt="Patanjali" width="330" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:Rpba · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patanjali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Several authors bear the name. The grammarian who wrote the Mahābhāṣya, a commentary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a>'s Aṣṭādhyāyī that also quotes Kātyāyana's vārttikas, is dated to the mid 2nd century BCE by Western and Indian scholars alike. The compiler of the Yoga Sutras is estimated to have lived between the 2nd century BCE and the 4th century CE, with many scholars narrowing it to the 2nd to 4th century CE. A medical text, the Patanjalatantra, is also quoted under the name, and a further Patanjali of about the 8th century wrote a commentary on the Charaka Samhita.</p><p>Louis Renou showed significant differences in language, grammar and vocabulary between the grammar and the yoga text. In 1914 James Wood proposed that they were the same person, and in 1922 Surendranath Dasgupta tentatively argued the same, but the view that they were different authors is now generally accepted by Western scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Yoga Sutras are 196 aphorisms on yoga. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> they were the most translated ancient Indian text, rendered into about forty Indian languages and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> and Arabic. The text fell into obscurity for nearly 700 years from the 12th century to the 19th and returned in the late 19th century through the efforts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> and others. Philipp Maas dates the text to about 400 CE from the commentaries on it, while Edwin Bryant reports that most scholars date it shortly after the turn of the Common Era.</p><p>In the Mahābhāṣya Patañjali holds that the word-meaning relation is natural, and defines an early notion of sphoṭa, the invariant quality of speech that stays the same across differences between speakers. The Chinese pilgrim I-tsing records that the Mahābhāṣya was studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with advanced scholars learning it in three years. Franz Kielhorn first edited it critically.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition Patanjali is regarded as an avatar of Adi Sesha, and in the Tamil Siddha tradition as one of eighteen siddhars. The Tamil Saiva tradition holds that he learned yoga from Nandhi Deva, and shrines at Tirupattur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> are said to hold his samadhi. The Mahābhāṣya made him the authority for more than 2,000 years as the last grammarian of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>.</p><p>Some modern schools of yoga, including Iyengar Yoga and Ashtanga Vinyasa Yoga, honour him with invocations and shrines. David Gordon White observes that teacher training often includes instruction in the Yoga Sutra, though he considers the text nearly devoid of discussion of postures, stretching and breathing and so largely irrelevant to yoga as now practised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patanjali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Patanjali</b>, around 150 BCE, wrote of the Yavanas besieging Saketa and Madhyamika, and the Yuga Purana prophesies that they would reach Pataliputra and cast down its mud walls before civil war called them home; whether such a campaign took place is disputed, and the Hathigumpha inscription has King…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Practices of yoga and meditation, rooted in Hindu traditions and the Yoga Sutras of <b>Patanjali</b>, have spread worldwide in adapted forms, and Gandhi’s non-violent resistance, inspired in part by the Bhagavad Gita, shaped movements for civil rights across the world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Later grammarians, Katyayana and <b>Patanjali</b>, commented on it, and Panini’s work fixed Classical Sanskrit in a form that changed little thereafter.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Gavin Flood gives about 600 to 300 BCE, Stephen Phillips 800 to 300 BCE, and Johannes Bronkhorst argues for a later date, possibly close to the grammarians Katyayana and <b>Patanjali</b>, around the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
